--- body ---

## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is rung 2 of five children, and 0026 is the tracker. Goal 2 is "it does not
lose or corrupt what it built" (README.md, "## The goals ladder"): three terms
that need a built index but not a good one, because a build that cannot survive
its own second day never reaches the rungs above.

Two of the three are this ticket's. R3 — what staying current costs is what
changed, never the size of the library — is the counter-churn clause, measured
by R17's own counters: edit one title and exactly the work that title implies
recomputes; resync identical bytes and nothing downstream of verification runs.
SPEC.md §5.2.8 calls this the test that would have caught the shipped 92,7 %
defect. R23 — an index under another schema version ends up serving, either
direction, no file deleted by hand — already has its damage-prevention half
running: `bench/smoke_upstream.py` restamps a copy of a built index in both
directions and asserts the foreign stamp is read before the file is opened
writable, with the results in `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json`. What is
missing is the serving half, which is the design's promise and upstream issue
\#34's, and which no restamp can establish. The third term, R13, is ticket 0035's.

The substrate exists. Tranche 1 (ticket 0551, closed) built the ledger schema,
the reconcile tick and the fixtures on the fork branch `conductor-integration`
(the integration base ruled in ticket 0568, closed), so the counters R3's
assertion reads are there to read. Nothing open blocks this ticket.

## Relevant files

- Fork branch `conductor-integration` — the substrate: tranches 1-3 and the v2
  content schema in one tree. `make upstream-checkout` recreates the git-ignored
  `fork/`; do not overwrite an existing checkout.
- `bench/smoke_upstream.py` — `_restamp_and_open`,
  `check_foreign_schema_is_sidelined`, `check_migration_absent`: R23's
  damage-prevention half, already asserted.
- `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/checks.json` — the verdict record.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**Counters (C4)**" — the two-axis work counters
  (`work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>`) R3's assertion is written against, and the
  `noop`/`done` distinction that makes the resync clause stateable.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.4 — freshness: how the index finds out what changed.
- `README.md`, "## Goal 2 — it does not lose or corrupt what it built".

## Actions

1. Build R3's assertion against a built index on the fork branch: edit exactly
   one title, then assert `work.record.edit.done == 1`,
   `work.embed.edit.done == sections(record)`, and every other `*.done` delta
   zero. §5.2.8 states the arithmetic; cite it rather than copying it.
2. The resync half: a simulated identical-bytes resync recomputes nothing. Every
   `*.done` delta is 0 and the touched items appear only under
   `work.*.resync.noop`. Verification itself runs and the gate MUST permit
   exactly that and nothing downstream of it.
3. R23's serving half. The restamp test says this build declines a foreign file;
   the promise is that the index ends up served. Assert the outcome — that a
   file stamped either way is being answered from, with no file deleted by hand
   — and where stock upstream does not yet do it, record the red honestly and
   name upstream issue \#34 as where it is filed. A waiver is a state, not a pass.
4. Both assertions run from the Makefile at goal 2's place in the gate estate.

## Test

Red first. For R3, the red fixture is the churn defect itself: a resync path
that recomputes on unchanged bytes must drive the assertion red, or the
assertion is watching the wrong counter. Build that arm before the green one —
a check written after the code it validates tends to be written against what the
code does. For R23's serving half the red state is the current one, and it has
to be seen as red rather than skipped: a gate that skips when the capability is
absent reports the same thing whether the capability is absent or the gate is
broken.

## Verification

- [ ] R3's edit assertion and its resync assertion each carry their own id and
      `falsified_by` string in the checks record.
- [ ] The churn arm was seen red before the clean arm was seen green.
- [ ] R23's serving assertion reports a state, not a skip, and names issue \#34.
- [ ] The counters read are the ones §5.2.8 declares; no counter name is
      invented here.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- R23's damage-prevention half already passes and must keep passing: this ticket
  adds the serving half and touches neither existing check.
- No threshold or design number is restated here; SPEC.md §5.2.8 owns them.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] R3's edit clause asserted against the built index, with a churn arm seen red
- [ ] R3's identical-bytes resync clause asserted, verification permitted and
      nothing downstream of it
- [ ] R23's serving clause asserted in both directions, its current state
      recorded honestly rather than skipped
- [ ] both run from the Makefile with honest red/waiver states
- [ ] README.md's goal-2 rows for R3 and R23 carry an evidence verdict that
      rests on something that ran
